"[2408.03803] Poisson Approximation of prime divisors of shifted primes (https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.03803)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.03803\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Number Theory\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 7 Aug 2024 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 19 Jan 2026 (this version, v4)]\nL9: # Title:Poisson Approximation of prime divisors of shifted primes\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Kevin Ford L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Poisson Approximation of prime divisors of shifted primes, by Kevin Ford\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We develop an analog for shifted primes of the Kubilius model of prime factors of integers. We prove a total variation distance estimate for the difference between the model and actual prime factors of shifted primes, and apply it to show that the prime factors of shifted primes in disjoint sets behave like independent Poisson variables. As a consequence, we establish a transference principle between the anatomy of random integers up to x and of random shifted primes p+a with p < x.\nL17: Comments:  | corrected a tiny issue in the proof of Lemma 3.3\nL18: Subjects:  | Number Theory (math.NT)\nL19: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 11N05, 11N25, 11N35, 11K65\nL20: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2408.03803 [math.NT]\nL21:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2408.03803v4 [math.NT] for this version)\nL22:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.03803†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Kevin Ford [cite12†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Wed, 7 Aug 2024 14:30